{"type": "spr", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A circle has center <math alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi>\n</math>, and points <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi>\n</math> lie on the circle. The measure of arc <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"45 degree\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the length of arc <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"4 pi\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> units. What is the length, in units, of the radius of the circle?</p>", "keys": ["16"], "rationale": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of arc <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 degree\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the length of arc <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 pi\"><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> units. In a circle, the circumference corresponds to an arc measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"360 degree\"><mn>360</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Therefore, the ratio of the measure of arc <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"360 degree\"><mn>360</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> is equal to the ratio of the length of arc&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> to the circumference. Since the circumference of a circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 pi r\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>,&nbsp;it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 45 Over 360 EndFraction equals StartFraction 4 pi Over 2 pi r EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>45</mn><mn>360</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. This equation can be rewritten as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one eighth equals StartFraction 2 Over r EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"8 r\"><mn>8</mn><mi>r</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1 left parenthesis r right parenthesis equals 2 left parenthesis 8 right parenthesis\"><mn>1</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r equals 16\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math>. Therefore, the length, in units, of the radius of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn></math>.</p>", "externalid": "c744c3b6-b2fb-433d-8650-de2dcf230cc9", "correct_answer": ["16"]}